package mop;

import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * SecretKeySpec
 *
 * A JavaMOP specification of the correct usage of the
 * javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKeySpec.crysl
 */
SecretKeySpecSpec(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec) {

   SecretKeySpec spec;

   // The second place of `generatedKey[this, alg]` (SecretKeySpec.cryptsl:41). The rule
   // binds `alg` in both constructors, so both events stage it beside the object, and the
   // handler writes the pair. It travels in a field for the same reason `spec` does: a
   // handler is generated as a no-argument method and sees no event parameter.
   String specAlgorithm;

   // One event over `SecretKeySpec(byte[], String)`, where there used to be two
   // split by the sign of a guard. The negated twin `c3` matched the same call with
   // the same `args(keyMaterial, keyAlgorithm)` and named no state in the `ere`, so
   // one bad construction drew two reports: a body accusation and an ordering one
   // about an ordering the rule accepts, because an event outside the automaton gets
   // a transition row that sends every state to the fail state. The fusion kept the
   // first and removed the second (task 3.4).
   //
   // This pass moves the read to the new store, and that is what closes the last
   // window the migration had left open. `randomized` is produced by `SecureRandom`,
   // which moved store at task 4.5, and this consumer was still reading the old one:
   // measured over the whole ErrorCollector, key material that a *observed*
   // SecureRandom had just filled was accused of not being observed to come from a
   // randomized source. One report becomes none, and the corpus trace
   // SecretKeySpecSpec.txt is that measurement's other half.
   //
   // The verdict is three-valued and the two accusing outcomes are different claims.
   // NOT_OBSERVED says no randomized source of this array was ever seen -- on Android
   // as often a reach limit of the instrumentation as a misuse -- and it is the one
   // a program can actually reach. VIOLATED means the array carries a randomization
   // predicate that was withdrawn, and nothing in api30 withdraws `randomized`, so
   // its code has no execution path; it is written anyway, for the reason the sibling
   // files record: a code that names a site is cheaper to have and not fire than to
   // add later, and the reason is on the record rather than in the reader's head.
   //
   // What the read tests is still not what the rule requires, and this pass does not
   // change that either. api30's SecretKeySpec REQUIRES `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial]`;
   // the read here is `randomized`, a different predicate that the seed conflated with
   // it. That is ledger clause #32, and it is un-conflated at task 5.10 together with
   // 6.1, where the real producer -- `SecretKeySpec.mop`'s `getEncoded` -- is wired.
   //
   // `spec` is bound only by the conforming branch, because `@match` turns it into the
   // rule's `generatedKey` and CrySL ensures a predicate only for a use that satisfied
   // the rule. A construction that broke the clause therefore reaches the handler with
   // nothing bound, and the write there is a no-op.
   event c1 after(byte[] keyMaterial, String keyAlgorithm) returning(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec):
    call(public SecretKeySpec.new(byte[], String))
    && args(keyMaterial, keyAlgorithm) {
      boolean conforms = true;
      PredicateVerdict randomisedMaterial = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.RANDOMIZED, keyMaterial);
      if (randomisedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='a randomized byte[]' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], String) carries a randomization predicate the rule does not admit'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      else if (randomisedMaterial == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-NOBS-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='a randomized byte[]' msg='the keyMaterial given to SecretKeySpec(byte[], String) was not observed to come from a randomized source'"));
          conforms = false;
      }
      if (conforms) {
          spec = secretKeySpec;
          specAlgorithm = keyAlgorithm;
      }
    }

   // The same fusion over the four-argument constructor. Here the twin `c4` was the
   // exact complement -- `keyMaterial.length < offset + len` against `>=` -- so the
   // two halves partition the join point and the fused body is one `if`/`else` over
   // the rule's only CONSTRAINTS clause, `length(keyMaterial) >= off + len`.
   //
   // This overload reads no predicate: api30 states one REQUIRES clause, over the key
   // material, and the four-argument constructor's material is the same array the
   // two-argument one takes -- but the rule binds `keyMaterial` in both events, so the
   // read belongs here too. It is not added in this pass because the clause it would
   // translate is the conflated one above, and adding a second conflated site would
   // double the work task 5.10 has to undo. The site is named in that task instead.
   //
   // The accusing half is unreachable, and is kept anyway. This is an
   // `after ... returning` advice, and the constructor rejects the combination with
   // an exception before it can return (`offset < 0`, `len < 0` and
   // `key.length - offset < len`, in that order; JDK 21
   // java.base/javax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec.java:148-168), so no execution
   // reaches SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 and no trace can be written that does. It stays
   // because it is the literal transcription of the clause the oracle states, and
   // because the API enforcing a rule's constraint itself is a fact about the
   // platform, not a reason for the specification to stop stating it.
   event c2 after(byte[] keyMaterial, int offset, int len, String keyAlgorithm) returning(SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec):
    call(public SecretKeySpec.new(byte[], int, int, String))
    && args(keyMaterial, offset, len, keyAlgorithm) {
      if (keyMaterial.length < offset + len) {
          ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
              "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='" + keyMaterial.length + "' exp='at least offset + len' msg='keyMaterial.length is smaller than offset + len'"));
      }
      else {
          spec = secretKeySpec;
          specAlgorithm = keyAlgorithm;
      }
    }

   // `Cons` is the whole of the rule's ORDER, and `Cons := c1 | c2`: constructing a
   // SecretKeySpec is the entire ordering this specification observes. So the `@fail`
   // handler below cannot fire. Measured in the generated monitor: the transition row
   // is {1, 2, 2} for both events and the monitor is keyed on the constructed object,
   // so no monitor of this specification ever sees a second event and state 2 is never
   // entered. SECRETKEYSPEC-ORDER-00 is therefore a third code with no execution path,
   // beside SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 and the VIOLATED branch of `c1`. This is the
   // position every constructor-only specification of the set is in, and the repair is
   // to the automaton: recorded here, owned by the task that repairs the family.
   ere : c1 | c2

   @fail {
      ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls, "SecretKeySpecSpec", "" + __LOC,
          "v=1 code=SECRETKEYSPEC-ORDER-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=SecretKeySpec val='' exp='' msg='the observed call sequence is not one SecretKeySpecSpec accepts'"));
      __RESET;
   }

   // The one place the rule's `ENSURES generatedKey[this, alg]` may be written. api30
   // states the clause without an `after L` qualification, so its acceptance point is
   // the accepting state itself, which in an `ere` is exactly what `@match` names.
   //
   // The write is now at the arity the rule states, two places over the object and its
   // algorithm, and it rises here in the same commit as both of its readers -- which is
   // the condition task 4.10 recorded when it left the write at one place. `validate`
   // compares the value tuple, so a two-place write read by a one-place `validate`
   // returns VIOLATED, not NOT_OBSERVED, and raising either side alone would turn every
   // SecretKeySpec-created key given to `Cipher.init` into a positive accusation about a
   // program that conforms. The readers that rise with it are `CipherSpec.i2`, which
   // applies the rule's `part(0,"/",transformation)` splitter, and `SecretKeySpec.e1`,
   // which asks with the key's own algorithm.
   //
   // `SecretKeySpecSpec-cipher-chain.txt` is the trace that measures exactly this, and
   // its header says so: the same conforming program that goes silent when producer and
   // consumer share a store would draw CIPHER-CONSTR-00 if only one of them moved arity.
   //
   // The rule's other ENSURES, `speccedKey[this, _]`, is not written at all. Its
   // consumer is SecretKeyFactory, which has no specification in this set; ledger #31
   // records the chain as `unmonitored-consumer` at task 5.10, and PBEKeySpec already
   // produces the predicate. A second producer for a chain with no consumer would be a
   // site with no purpose, and no read is fabricated to give it one.
   //
   // The accepting-state bookkeeping that stood beside this write is gone: the new
   // store does not offer it, and the set never read it back (INV-INS-147).
   @match {
      PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_KEY, spec, specAlgorithm);
   }

}
